WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:02.550
- [Narrator] This submarine
is what experts are calling

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:04.710
the future of underwater defense.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.450
It's a nuclear powered attack sub

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.610
that combines next-gen UK design

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:10.770
with classified US technology,

00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.150
and it's being built in Australia

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.340
where it'll be deployed in the 2040s.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.810
- The US doesn't usually share

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.700
this type of technology with its allies,

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.910
so the fact that Australia's
getting it is a massive deal.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.050
- [Narrator] But the future subs,

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:25.440
which are estimated to
cost up to $250 billion,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:27.810
have been criticized by China and Russia,

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.460
who say the vessels risk sparking

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.980
a nuclear arms race in the Indo-Pacific.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.720
So here's the secret US technology

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:36.450
that will enhance these subs,
and how it might be used

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:38.433
to counter China's growing influence.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.090
The SSN-AUKUS is being built

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:45.300
with one key feature in
mind, interoperability.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.160
That's the ability for all three nations

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.780
to integrate the same technology,

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.230
weapons, and nuclear
reactors into their subs.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:53.850
To do this, the US will share

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:56.580
its highly classified
nuclear submarine technology,

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.280
making the UK and Australia
the only two nations

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.650
to ever publicly receive these systems.

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.570
Australia will also
become the seventh country

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:05.370
in the world to own these vessels,

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:06.720
which have greater capabilities

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.180
than their current
diesel-electric submarines.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.390
- They can go farther, they
can generally go faster

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:13.860
and they're quieter as well,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.350
so there's a big capability advantage

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.270
in a maritime environment.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.130
- [Narrator] Naval experts
say the submarine's design

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.810
will likely feature a larger hull

00:01:21.810 --> 00:01:23.610
than Australia's current submarines,

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:26.160
housing almost twice as many crew members.

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:29.010
That crew will operate US
combat control systems,

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.930
which detect the submarine's surroundings

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.240
and direct its weapons to their targets.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.040
Alongside US torpedoes,

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.840
those weapons will include missiles fired

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.820
from vertical launch systems.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:41.220
Experts say these multipurpose
silos will be similar

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.994
to the modules used in US
Virginia class submarines

00:01:44.994 --> 00:01:46.290
(explosion bangs and hisses)

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:49.290
allowing all three nations
to use the same US tomahawk

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:51.030
and hypersonic missiles.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.900
- Strapping a American missile
into an Australian submarine

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:56.970
and having it work absolutely seamlessly

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.210
would be a great advantage
in any military conflict.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.160
- [Narrator] All of these
weapons will be conventional,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.290
as Australia doesn't have nuclear weapons.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.300
That means building the
nuclear reactors needed

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:09.330
to power these subs on its
own shores could violate

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.540
the 1968 Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty.

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.280
The treaty limits the transfer and use

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.580
of highly enriched uranium
to non-nuclear weapon states,

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.190
so Australia will use what
critics have called a loophole

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.380
for the first time in
the treaty's history.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.780
The power units will
instead be built in the UK

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:27.480
by Rolls Royce and
enhanced with US technology

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.160
to make them safer and quieter.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.570
They'll then be completely sealed

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:32.730
before being shipped to Australia.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.100
This arrangement has sparked
concerns from campaigners

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.680
who want to stop the
spread of nuclear weapons,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.555
and drawn criticism from China and Russia.

00:02:40.555 --> 00:02:44.472
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:49.980
- China has said this could lead

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:52.500
to an arms race in the region.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.400
Obviously the US and
Australia deny those claims,

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:59.370
but Russia and China do
not like this deal at all.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:01.650
- [Narrator] That's because of
tensions in the Indo-Pacific,

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.680
where the AUKUS Alliance says
these subs will be deployed.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.200
Experts say choke points,
including the Sunda Strait

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.990
and Taiwan Strait, as well
as the South China Sea,

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.780
are the primary zones of
interest for these subs.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:15.510
- The way that the US is
seeking to counter China

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.820
in the region is that
they're trying to build up

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.070
the capabilities of their allies,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.100
and this is one of the ways
that the US is doing that.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:23.933
- [Narrator] But critics say

00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:26.040
that all this advanced
technology could be obsolete

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:29.430
by the time the SSN-AUKUS
is actually deployed.

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.460
Experts have pointed to the
rapid development of UUVs,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.350
or unmanned underwater vehicles,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.110
as both a challenge to the
importance of submarines

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:39.780
and an opportunity for
their continued relevance.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.600
- You might have one-man
submarines sort of controlling

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.820
a fleet of underwater drones,

00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:47.100
but when you're looking
at such a big timeframe,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.390
20 years in the future,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:52.500
it's unclear how much
of a need there will be.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:53.550
- [Narrator] That uncertainty

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.250
and the significant price
tag attached to these vessels

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.470
has raised the eyebrows of critics,

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.050
but experts say the high
cost shows how important

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.300
a stronger presence in the Indo-Pacific is

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:04.800
for the AUKUS Alliance.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.120
- There is a growing recognition

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.800
that the US cannot do this alone,

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.900
China's been pouring a
lot of money and time

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.370
into its military buildup,
and to keep pace with China,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.097
the US is going to have to
seek help from its allies

00:04:17.097 --> 00:04:18.630
and Australia really is

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:20.630
one of its biggest allies in the region.

